So I believe I am getting the hang of calling CNVs accurately via codex, however in a recent study I am I noticed that I have some genes/targets with exremely high likelihood ratios as opposed to my positive controls. Those same CNV calls seem to be repeated throughout a high percentage of my samples. Does it become more difficult for Codex to distinguish between FP/TP with a higher number of total samples (600+) or would those repeated extreme likelihood values possibly reflect some sort of batch effect not compensated for in PCA? Would the solution be to increase my k to remove that component if so?

On Monday, April 3, 2017 at 11:17:45 AM UTC-4, n.b....@gmail.com wrote:
> Apparently I have been using a target bed file that is sorted for a different algorithm which has been creating some issues. I believe the issue is resolved now as I am not observing any incorrect annotations or negative length exons.
